const title       = 'Do Ravens Get Lonely? Social Needs and Isolation in Ravens';
const description = 'Ravens are highly social animals with complex relationships. Research suggests ravens show signs of distress when isolated and form strong pair and alliance bonds. This page examines what science says about raven social needs and loneliness.';
const path        = '/questions/do-ravens-get-lonely/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Do Ravens Get Lonely?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Are ravens social animals?', a: 'Yes, ravens are highly social. They live in a complex social world: breeding pairs maintain long-term bonds; non-breeding birds form communal flocks with stable social hierarchies and individual relationships; and ravens form alliance partnerships — consistent non-kin allies who groom, play with, and support each other in social conflicts. Ravens have sophisticated social cognition including third-party knowledge of others\' relationships — they appear to track who is allied with whom.' },
  { q: 'Do captive ravens need companionship?', a: 'Yes. Ravens kept in isolation from conspecifics and with limited social interaction from humans show behavioural abnormalities consistent with stress and understimulation — excessive calling, stereotyped movements, feather damage. Well-established guidelines for raven care in captivity (zoos, wildlife rehabilitation) emphasise social contact as a welfare requirement. A single captive raven needs either raven companionship or intensive, frequent human social interaction to meet its social needs.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens form friendships?', a: 'The concept of "friendship" in animals is scientifically defined as a stable, equitable, long-term affiliation between specific individuals. By this definition, ravens form friendships. Research by Bugnyar and colleagues at the Konrad Lorenz Research Centre (Austria) has documented non-kin alliance partnerships in ravens that are stable over years, involve mutual support in social conflicts, and show reciprocal behaviour — birds that helped allies received help in return. These are the defining characteristics of animal friendship as studied in primates.' },
];
